const p = this.p, j = x >>> (32 - p), w = (x << p) >>> 0;
const r = w === 0 ? 32 - p + 1 : Math.clz32(w) + 1;
if (r > this.M[j]) { this.M[j] = r; return true; }
```
The unsigned shift >>> keeps j in 0 to 4,095. x << p moves the 20 remaining bits to the top so Math.clz32 counts their leading zeros, and >>> 0 reads the result as unsigned. If w is 0, clz32 would return 32 and count the 12 shifted in zeros as signal, so the rank is capped at 32 - p + 1 = 21. The suite pins the edges with crafted hashes: 0xFFF00000 lands in register 4,095 with rank 21, 0x00080000 in register 0 with rank 1, and 0x00100001 in register 1 with rank 20. Another test adds 500 keys, adds them again five times, and requires an unchanged estimate, which is the idempotence property distinct counting needs.
